{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "שא נא כליך", :translit "sa na kalekha", :gloss "lift now your weapons"} {:he "משיאה על גבי חברתה", :translit "masi'ah al gabei khaverta", :gloss "raising one upon its companion"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "lexical: שא read as שֵׁשׂ / sharpening, as in the phrase משיאה על גבי חברתה"}], :frame {:text "The verb שא, normally read as 'lift,' is here read with the sense of שֵׁשׂ, to sharpen or whet a blade, as when one raises a sword against another (the phrase משיאה על גבי חברתה appears in contexts of drawing or crossing blades).", :sources ["Genesis 27:3" "Mishnah Shabbat 17:3 (mashiah al gabei khavertah)"]}} {:quotes [{:he "וצא", :translit "vetze", :gloss "and go out"} {:he "וצא השדה", :translit "vetze ha-sadeh", :gloss "and go out to the field"} {:he "וצא הלחם", :translit "vetze ha-lachem", :gloss "and go out for bread"} {:he "כליך", :translit "kalekha", :gloss "your weapons"} {:he "תליך", :translit "talekha", :gloss "your quiver"} {:he "קשתך", :translit "keshetkha", :gloss "your bow"} {:he "השדה", :translit "ha-sadeh", :gloss "the field"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["וצא השדה" "וצא הלחם"]}], :frame {:text "Bereshit Rabbah reads each item Isaac names — כליך (your weapons), תליך (your quiver), קשתך (your bow), השדה (the field) — as an allusion to one of the four kingdoms: Babylon, Media, Greece, and Edom. The tradition that the Holy One wages war on Israel's behalf against these empires is expressed in the verse 'HaShem is a man of war' (Exodus 15:3). Amalek's defeat is foretold in 'I will utterly blot out the memory of Amalek' (Exodus 17:14) and 'you shall blot out the memory of Amalek' (Deuteronomy 25:19).", :sources ["Genesis 27:3" "Bereshit Rabbah 65:13" "Exodus 15:3" "Exodus 17:14" "Deuteronomy 25:19"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ציד", :translit "tzayid", :gloss "game"} {:he "צידה", :translit "tzayidah", :gloss "game (with extra heh)"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "extra heh in צידה teaches five halakhot of slaughter and five signs of kashrut"}], :frame {:text "The consonantal text of Genesis 27:3 spells ציד (without a final heh) yet is read as צידה. The word ציד without the heh already carries the sense of hunted food (cf. Genesis 25:27–28). The signs of purity for birds are enumerated in Mishnah Chullin 3:6, and for wild animals in Leviticus 11:3–6 and Deuteronomy 14:6–8.", :sources ["Genesis 27:3" "Genesis 25:27" "Genesis 25:28" "Leviticus 11:3" "Mishnah Chullin 3:6" "Mishnah Chullin 3:7" "Deuteronomy 14:6"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "lexical: שא read as שֵׁשׂ / sharpening, as in the phrase משיאה על גבי חברתה", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["וצא השדה" "וצא הלחם"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "וצא השדה", :refs ["Genesis 27:3"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "וצא הלחם", :refs ["Exodus 17:9"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "extra heh in צידה teaches five halakhot of slaughter and five signs of kashrut", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:08:36.603628921Z", :model "glm-5.2"}